{0 Dynamic linking and dynaming loading}

@author Etienne Millon

Ctypes manipulates two related concepts: {e dynamic linking} and
{e dynamic loading}. These are related, but different. Understanding the
difference might help answer the question, "Why can't ctypes find my symbols?".

This document is specific to Linux.

{1 Dynamic linking}

{e Dynamic linking} is the mechanism to use {e dynamic libraries} (or rather,
{e dynamically-linked libraries}).

Libraries, especially those written in C, can be linked in two different ways.

Static linking will copy the contents of the library into each executable.
Statically linked libraries use extension [.a].

In contrast, dynamic linking will only record a link in each executable's
header. Dynamically linked libraries use extension [.so]. These links are
resolved by the dynamic loader ([ld-linux.so]) when the program starts.

When dynamic linking is used, functions from the external library look like
normal functions in C. For example, [png_init_io] from [libpng] is used no
differently than [memcpy].

{1 Dynamic loading}

{e Dynamic loading} is the mechanism to use {e plugins}.

These are object files too and have extension [.so]. However, their purpose is
different. Their purpose is to extend an executable with code that is either
optional or not available at the time the executable is linked.

At runtime, code from plugins is referenced through the [dlopen] and [dlsym]
functions, and through function pointers.

In the context of [ctypes], {!Foreign} from the [ctypes-foreign] library relies
on this mechanism to locate functions defined in shared objects.

{1 A closer look}

These two mechanisms are similar, and actually rely on the same underlying
tools: the ELF file format used by [.so] files contains a list of symbols that
are queried by the dynamic loader and by [dlsym].

It is actually possible to dynamically load a dynamic library using [dlopen] (in
the context of [ctypes], via {!Dl.dlopen}) and refer to code defined in it using
[dlsym] (in the context of [ctypes], via {!Foreign.foreign}). But doing so
requires knowing the path to the right [.so] file, and applications usually are
not concerned with that (in contrast applications know where to look for
plugins).

As a special case, it is possible to pass [RTLD_DEFAULT] to [dlsym] instead of
an object file (in the context of [ctypes], this corresponds to not passing
[?from] to {!Foreign.foreign}). In that case, the symbol will be searched in
"the default shared object search order". This includes all the global symbols
in the executable and the loaded dynamic libraries, but as we will see in the
next section, that might not be a good idea.

{1 Why can't ctypes find my symbols ?}

A common failure mode of [ctypes] looks like the following. Our goal is to
define an OCaml wrapper around a C library like [libpng].

First, we define a library that is dynamically linked against [libpng]:

{@dune[
  (library
   (name opng)
   (c_library_flags :standard -lpng))
]}

This library calls {!Foreign.foreign}:

{[
  let version_number =
    let open Ctypes in
    Foreign.foreign "png_access_version_number" (void @-> returning uint32_t)
]}

Then, we use this library in an executable:

{@dune[
  (executable
   (name opng_demo)
   (libraries opng))
]}

{[
  let () =
    let v = Opng.version_number () in
    print_endline (Unsigned.UInt32.to_string v)
]}

And the symbol cannot be found - how come?

{@text[
  Fatal error: exception Dl.DL_error("_build/default/opng_demo.exe: undefined symbol: png_access_version_number")
]}

{2 Technical explanation}

Let's first explain why this does not work. When [-lpng] is added to the C flags
in the library, it gets recorded in the [.cmxa] file as an "Extra C object
file". This means that in turn, it will be passed by [ocamlopt] to the linker
when producing the final executable.

However, at that point the linker can see that [-lpng] is passed but that the
executable uses no symbol from this library (no entry exists in the symbol
tables). It means that it can choose not to link the library. Historically,
linkers would keep it, but since around 2012, Linux distributions have started
enabling this this behavior (controlled by the [--as-needed] linker flag).

As a consequence, the executable is not dynamically linked against [libpng], as
can be seen by inspecting the output of [ldd _build/default/onpg_demo.exe]. This
explains why the symbol can not be found at runtime: it has not been loaded by
the dynamic loader.

{2 Moral explanation}

Another way to see this is that [ctypes-foreign] and {!Foreign} is adapted to
plugins (and dynamic loading), not writing library bindings (for dynamic
linking).

{2 Workarounds}

It is possible to alter a bit the project to ensure that the library stays
linked. One solution is to reference a C symbol through an [external]. For
example:

{[
  external _force_link : unit -> unit = "png_chunk_error"
]}

Note that the symbol does not have to be used in the OCaml library, and the type
does not have to match what the symbol does. Actually, calling it is likely to
crash since this function does not expect OCaml values as arguments.

Another way to fix it is to change the linker flags to pass [--no-as-needed]
before [-lpng], for example by using
[(flags :standard -ccopt -Wl,--no-as-needed)].

These workarounds might not be portable or break with future evolutions of the
OCaml or C toolchains.

{2 Solution}

Stub generation through {!Cstubs} is a better fit for this.
